Quick legal summary (what the law says)

Easement (right of way) — under the Indian Easements Act, 1882 a landowner may have an easement (for example, a right of way) over another’s land. This is the usual legal mechanism to get access across someone else’s property. 
India Code

Easement of necessity — if your parcel is truly landlocked (no possible access to a public road except by crossing another parcel), the law recognises an easement of necessity that can be claimed because the land cannot be used otherwise. (This is narrower than “convenience” — the access must be necessary.) 
