Practical options (ranked from simplest to most adversarial)
1) Negotiate and register an easement (recommended start)

Approach the layout owner / developer or the adjacent plot owners and negotiate a written easement/right-of-way agreement that: defines the exact route, width, permitted uses (pedestrian/vehicle), maintenance obligations, and compensation (if any).

Important: get the easement deed drafted by a lawyer and register it at the local sub-registrar so it binds future owners. A written, registered easement is the cleanest, fastest, enforceable solution. 
ClearTax

2) Ask the local authority / municipality to intervene

If the layout was approved without providing mandatory access or if a public approach was blocked improperly, you can file a complaint with the municipal corporation / panchayat / revenue office asking them to (a) enforce layout approvals, (b) open/dedicate a public approach road, or (c stop illegal obstruction. Municipalities sometimes resolve these without litigation. 
PathLegal

3) Document use and pursue prescriptive easement (if you already used the route)

If you and predecessors used the path openly, peacefully and as of right for a long period (commonly ~20 years), you may be able to claim a prescriptive easement. This requires strong documentary and witness evidence and usually litigation. 
