5) Purchase or swap land / acquire narrow strip

If the owners are willing, acquiring a small strip or buying a corner can permanently solve the problem. This is often the simplest commercially if the landowner will sell.

What you should collect now (checklist)

Title documents / sale deed for your plot (showing its boundaries).

Old maps, revenue records, mutation entries, and layout plan for the neighbourhood. (Check whether a road was intended on the original layout plan.)

Any evidence of historical access — photos, dated witness statements, bills, letters showing you or predecessors used the path.

Survey sketch (to be prepared by a licensed surveyor) showing proposed route and neighbouring parcel numbers.

Correspondence (emails/WhatsApp) or notices you’ve sent/received regarding access.

Owner details of the abutting plots / layout developer and municipal approvals for the layout.

These documents will help in negotiation, municipal complaints, or court proceedings. 
